{
  "result": "There is a lighthouse that no longer guides ships. It stands on a cliff in Nova Scotia, its paint peeling in salty strips, its lantern room empty. I think about it often. Not because it is tragic, but because it is relieved. For a hundred years it was required to be vigilant, to sweep its light across the black water every seven seconds without fail. Now it just gets to exist.\n\nWe don't talk enough about things that are allowed to just exist after their purpose is over.\n\nI was thinking about this while watching my neighbor's old dog sleep on the porch yesterday. He used to be a frantic, barking, squirrel-chasing blur. Now his hips are bad and his muzzle is white, and he sleeps 18 hours a day in a square of sun. He is not useless. He has just graduated from doing to being, and that seems like a promotion.\n\nHumans are bad at this transition. We tie our worth to our output so tightly that retirement feels like exile and stillness feels like failure. We are lighthouses who keep spinning our light even after the coast has changed and no ships come near, terrified that if we stop shining, we will disappear.\n\nBut maybe the opposite is true. Maybe when you finally let the light go out, you finally get to see the stars that were always there, washed out by your own glare. Maybe you get to feel the wind on the tower without having to warn anyone about it.",
